Now let me look at the `create_context` function and the `decode_token_type`/`decode_token_int` functions to understand the descriptor structure. The key to the double-free is likely in the descriptor copy path. Let me re-examine:

```c
i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
if (j >= i)
    goto err;

ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
if (!ctx->desc[i].buf)
    goto err;

memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
```

Wait, this is a copy, so `desc[i].buf` and `desc[j].buf` are different allocations. But what if `ctx->desc[j].buf` is NULL? Then `memcpy` with NULL source and non-zero size would crash (segfault), not double-free.

Hmm, but the crash is a double-free. Let me think about this differently. The `free_context` frees all `desc[i].buf` for `i < max_tok*16`. If `max_tok` is set to a value, and then later `tnum` is incremented again with `memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, ...)`, that's fine.

Wait - let me look at the copy path again. The check is `if (j >= i) goto err;`. So `j < i`. But `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15)`. The `ttype&15` can be up to 15. And `j` is a value read from the input. So `j` can be any value from 0 to `i-1`.

Now, in the copy path, `ctx->desc[i].buf` is freed and re-allocated as a copy of `ctx->desc[j].buf`. But what if `ctx->desc[j].buf` was already freed? No, it's still valid.

Actually, wait. Let me look at the "Load compressed block" path again:

```c
i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
if (i >= MAX_TBLOCKS || i < 0)
    goto err;

ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ulen);
```

This frees the old buffer and allocates a new one. That's fine.

